
Single versus multi user

Unix "comes up" in single-user mode when it
is booted. To enter multi-user mode, simply enter
"init 2" and hit return. Ultimately you will see the "login:" prompt.

Certain ports are "enabled" as login ports when the system commences
multi-user mode. This is governed by the /etc/inittab file. The relevant lines
in that file are:

co::respawn:/etc/getty console co_9600
t0::off:/etc/getty tty0 co_9600
t1::respawn:/etc/getty tty1 co_9600

This means that login is enabled for the console (the builtin Lisa terminal) -
which is the "co" entry, and for the "t1" entry" which is known to
Unix as /dev/tty1 - the "serial b" port on the back of the Lisa.

As we supply the Lisa Unix System V to you, /dev/tty0, which is
labelled Serial A on the Lisa is NOT a login port.

To change the entries, use the editor and adjust the "respawn" and "off"
items as needed. You then must reboot the Lisa for the
changes to take affect.

By the way, it is possible to have the Lisa come up in multi-user mode.
To do so, change the "s" entry in the "initdefault" line to "2".

PLEASE READ AND UNDERSTAND THE MATERIAL ON /ETC/INITTAB IN SECTION 4
OF THE MANUAL BEFORE ALTERING THIS FILE.
